<UserControl xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             x:Class="CcpClient.Desktop.Views.Pages.PlayPage">
  <!--
    The Play page and its DTRH hero card (wpf-surface-reachability.md §3 and §8.5; source at
    Views/Tabs/PlayTabView.xaml). Hop 2 of the port's flagship route: the rail door NAVIGATES
    here, and the one window-opening button lives on this page — WPF's one-entry rule
    (MainWindow/MainWindow.Presets.cs:1007,1036).

    THE CARD TAKES THE CLICK. Nothing here is ever disabled. WPF's lock band is a scrim, not a
    curtain: IsHitTestVisible="False" so the press passes straight through and TierGate.DemandLab
    inside the handler does the refusing (PlayTabView.xaml:508-512, style :251-258, and the
    band's own comment at :503-506 — "a band that swallowed the click would be a dead end").
    The one genuinely disabled part in WPF is the checkbox pair, and only because it writes
    settings through a binding with no handler to refuse in (MainWindow/MainWindow.PlayTab.cs:88-91).

    WHAT IS OMITTED, AND WHY (divergences §9 D18, D19). WPF's card also carries "Announcements"
    and "3D game (recommended)" checkboxes (PlayTabView.xaml:488,494) and a NEW pill (:432).
    Neither setting is ported, and unticking the 3D box selects a classic WPF descent the port
    does not have — so rendering them would be dead dials, which is the greyed control that
    swallows the gesture (the §9 D7 rule). They are absent, not disabled.
  -->
  <StackPanel Margin="20" Spacing="4">
    <TextBlock Classes="page-title" Text="Play" />
    <!-- The page's own live sub-line (§8.5). -->
    <TextBlock Classes="page-blurb" Text="Games, modes, and the deep end." />

    <Border Classes="hero" Margin="0,16,0,0">
      <Panel>
        <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto">
          <StackPanel Grid.Column="0" VerticalAlignment="Center" Spacing="10">
            <!-- The tier livery, top-left on this hero exactly as in WPF
                 (PlayTabView.xaml:398-411, Tier="2"). The words are the badge ART's words
                 (Controls/TierBadge.cs:21 — "BASIC SUBJECT" / "PRIME SUBJECT" are baked into
                 the PNGs, which is why the feature adds no localisation keys). The literal
                 string "TIER 2" appears nowhere in the running app and must not appear here.
                 It is a PRICE TAG on the feature, never a claim about the person reading it:
                 the badge "reads the tier, never decides it" (TierBadge.cs:24). -->
            <Border Classes="tier-badge" HorizontalAlignment="Left">
              <TextBlock x:Name="TierBadgeText"
                         Classes="tier-badge-text"
                         AutomationProperties.AutomationId="TierBadgeText"
                         Text="PRIME SUBJECT" />
            </Border>

            <!-- PlayTabView.xaml:426, emoji-stripped (the §9 D8 rule: the app strips emoji
                 mod-aware, MainWindow/MainWindow.UiUpdates.cs:101,124). §8.5 of the evidence
                 doc read this as "THE RABBIT HOLE" from a capture in which the onboarding card
                 and the badge occluded the left of the title; that reading is corrected in the
                 doc by this packet. -->
            <TextBlock x:Name="HeroTitle"
                       Classes="hero-title"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="HeroTitle"
                       Text="DOWN THE RABBIT HOLE" />

            <!-- PlayTabView.xaml:435, verbatim. Deliberately never localized upstream. -->
            <TextBlock Classes="page-blurb" MaxWidth="520" HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                       Text="it's right there. it's always been right there. bubbles drift up carrying flashes, videos, overlays. snap them in time or let them trigger you. stack streaks, accept mantras, sink deeper." />
          </StackPanel>

          <StackPanel Grid.Column="1" VerticalAlignment="Center" Margin="24,0,0,0" Spacing="8">
            <!-- PlayTabView.xaml:455,458 — the pink primary, and its tooltip verbatim. -->
            <Button x:Name="FallInButton"
                    Classes="hero-primary"
                    AutomationProperties.AutomationId="FallInButton"
                    AutomationProperties.Name="FALL IN"
                    ToolTip.Tip="you were always going to."
                    Content="FALL IN" />
            <!-- PlayTabView.xaml:468-469 — outlined, skips the picker, reuses the last slot. -->
            <Button x:Name="QuickDropButton"
                    Classes="hero-secondary"
                    AutomationProperties.AutomationId="QuickDropButton"
                    AutomationProperties.Name="Quick Drop"
                    ToolTip.Tip="skip the dollhouse. fall straight in with your saved settings"
                    Content="Quick Drop" />
          </StackPanel>
        </Grid>

        <!-- The refusal band. WPF's colours: scrim #A8120A1E ("~66% so the card art still reads
             through it. Seeing what you are missing is the entire job." PlayTabView.xaml:247-248),
             tier-2 rim #FFB47BFF (:245), corner 15 = the card's 16 minus its 1px border (:252).

             IsHitTestVisible="False" is declared on the ELEMENT, not tucked into a style, because
             it is the load-bearing attribute of the whole surface (PlayTabView.xaml:257): a band
             that took the click would swallow the gesture and tell the user nothing.

             DIVERGENCE §9 D16: WPF paints its band from the tier it already knows, BEFORE any
             click, and the band reads "Lab only". The port cannot know a tier without an async
             read, and a band painted from a cached read would advertise a state nobody
             re-checked — so this band appears when a press is refused and carries the refusal
             itself. §9 D17: WPF's refusal is additionally an 8-second Warning toast with a
             "See tiers" action (Services/TierGate.cs:133); the port has neither a toast system
             nor an App Info & Data page. -->
        <Border x:Name="GateBand"
                Classes="lock-band"
                IsHitTestVisible="False"
                IsVisible="False">
          <StackPanel VerticalAlignment="Center" Spacing="8" Margin="24">
            <TextBlock x:Name="GateBandTitle"
                       Classes="lock-title"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="GateBandTitle"
                       HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                       Text="" />
            <TextBlock x:Name="GateBandText"
                       Classes="lock-text"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="GateBandText"
                       HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                       TextAlignment="Center"
                       TextWrapping="Wrap"
                       MaxWidth="620"
                       Text="" />
          </StackPanel>
        </Border>
      </Panel>
    </Border>
  </StackPanel>
</UserControl>
